// What is in the repository and reaches nothing: unused files, unused dependencies, unused exports.
//
// One run at the root covers all four workspace members, which is why this is a root config and a
// root `knip` script rather than a per-member one. Knip reads `pnpm-workspace.yaml` itself and
// analyses each member in its own right, so a dependency listed in `apps/web` and used only in
// `scripts` is still reported.
//
// The baseline was made clean rather than ignored — CAN-61 Keep the codebase and its dependencies
// from silting up requires that, on the reasoning that a report nobody expects to be empty is a
// report nobody reads. Every override below is something that could not be fixed at the source, and
// each says why. Where this runs in CI, and why it runs where it does:
// docs/agents/workflow.md -> The gates.
{
  // `schema-jsonc.json` rather than `schema.json`: knip ships both, and the JSONC one is the schema
  // for this file type — it is what allows the comments above and below.
  "$schema": "https://unpkg.com/knip@6/schema-jsonc.json",
  "workspaces": {
    "scripts": {
      // Two entry points knip cannot find for itself. It resolves `check-docs.ts` through its
      // GitHub Actions plugin, which reads `.github/workflows/ci.yml` and sees a step run it — and
      // naming that one here as well is reported as redundant. The two named here run by hand and
      // no workflow reaches them, so knip reads each as a file nothing uses:
      // `provision-provider-repository.ts`, run once per Provider repository
      // (docs/infrastructure.md -> The Provider repository baseline), and
      // `check-linear-bodies.ts`, run either side of a Linear body write
      // (docs/agents/issue-tracker.md -> Keep an emphasis run on one line, and check the stored
      // body). Neither can be a CI gate: `orca` is a local desktop CLI.
      //
      // The other two are the worktree-database pair
      // (docs/adr/0025-a-preview-database-per-worktree.md). `provision-worktree-database.ts` is run
      // by `orca.yaml`'s `scripts.setup`, which is Orca's file rather than a workflow, and
      // `sweep-worktree-databases.ts` is run from `/review-pr`. Both are reached from prose and a
      // YAML key knip has no plugin for, so both read as files nothing uses.
      //
      // `restore-database.ts` is the fifth, and it is a file nothing may ever run automatically:
      // restoring a backup needs the age identity, which is on a laptop and in no workflow by
      // design (docs/adr/0028-a-nightly-encrypted-backup-off-neon.md). `backup-database.ts` is not
      // here because the GitHub Actions plugin finds it in `backup-database.yml`, the same way it
      // finds `check-docs.ts`.
      //
      // This declares entry points rather than suppressing a report. A genuinely dead file added
      // to this workspace later is still reported, which an `ignore` entry would have stopped.
      "entry": [
        "provision-provider-repository.ts",
        "check-linear-bodies.ts",
        "provision-worktree-database.ts",
        "restore-database.ts",
        "sweep-worktree-databases.ts"
      ],

      // The first entry in this file's only ignore list, and docs/agents/workflow.md -> The gates
      // records why the list now exists at all. `orca` is the Orca desktop app's own CLI, installed
      // with the app and not by any package manager, so there is no dependency for knip to resolve
      // it against and no version this repository could pin: the report is true and unfixable at the
      // source, which is what separates it from the `pnpm -r typecheck` case that workflow.md
      // rejected an entry for.
      //
      // Declared rather than worked around. `check-docs.ts` calls the same binary and escapes the
      // check only because the name reaches `execFileSync` through a helper's parameter instead of
      // as a literal — an accident of shape, not a statement about the binary, and not a pattern to
      // copy in order to stay quiet.
      "ignoreBinaries": ["orca"]
    },
    "apps/web": {
      // The drizzle plugin's whole job is to load `drizzle.config.ts` and read the `schema` and
      // `out` paths out of it. It cannot here: that file throws at import when
      // `MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL` is unset, deliberately, because a migration run against no
      // credential is worse than one that stops. Nobody holds that credential on a laptop and the
      // CI job holds it only on `main`, so the plugin would error on almost every run.
      //
      // So the plugin is told to load nothing, and the two paths it would have derived are named
      // here instead. `schema.ts` is the load-bearing one: drizzle-kit reads it through the config
      // rather than through an import, so without it every `pgRole` and `pgEnum` in that file
      // reads as an export nothing consumes.
      //
      // **What this costs: every export in `schema.ts` is exempt, not only the ones drizzle-kit
      // reaches.** An entry file is a root of the graph, so a genuinely dead export added to that
      // file later will not be reported. That is worth knowing and is not worth fixing here,
      // because it is what a working plugin does too — `resolveConfig` returns
      // `[config.schema].flat().map(id => toProductionEntry(id))`
      // (`knip/dist/plugins/drizzle/index.js`), so the exemption is knip's design for drizzle
      // schemas rather than something this override introduces. Naming the file here reproduces
      // the plugin's output exactly; it does not widen it.
      "drizzle": { "config": [] },

      // The third is `lighthouserc.cjs`, added by CAN-60 Gate the front end on bytes, budgets and
      // React lint, and it is here for a plainer reason than the two above: knip ships no plugin
      // for Lighthouse CI, and `lhci` finds its configuration by looking for a file of that name
      // rather than by anything importing it (`@lhci/utils/src/lighthouserc.js` holds the list of
      // ten names it tries). So nothing in the repository references it and knip is right that
      // nothing does.
      //
      // An entry rather than an ignore, for the reason the `scripts` block above gives: this file
      // is a root of the graph, so what it reaches stays analysed.
      "entry": ["drizzle.config.ts", "src/db/schema.ts", "lighthouserc.cjs"]
    }
  }
}
